1. Description

This is a log parser that generates a report based on a log file, allowing the analyst to quickly discern if a brute force attack is in place, as well as helpful statistics about attacking IPs and targeted users.

2. How to Use It

From command line, call
    python auth_scanner.py

Once called, enter file location (full path if not in same folder)

3. Implementation Details

    2 part timestamp parsed with using line.split to separate into parts, and taking the first two parts (the timestamp) and splitting them on the space into the full timestamp. If there are not at least 2 parts in the line it will skip that line. During the validation, if there is no timestamp it also skips the line.

    Counter is designed for tallying hashable objects, like IPs and Usernames, thus making it a cleaner approach to counting in this case. Easier to add to than iterating over dictionary keys

    Malformed logs are skipped if too malformed, but if containing the necessary fields than a warning about malformed log is displayed and the required data is still parsed

4. Error Handling

Can handle edge cases such as:
    Empty lines
    Missing timestamp
    Malformed key=value pairs
    Missing required fields
    File not found
    Ran on empty file and tried to divide by 0

For severely malformed lines, discards line and updates error count

5. AI Usage

I did not use AI tools

6. Challenges

Biggest challenge was getting error handling to function, because there was so many different types of errors in the log file that might appear. I solved this by slowing down and working each error one at a time before orchestrating that handling together into the parse_log_line function